# RemoteJobs44 — Native app (React Native / Expo)

A React Native port of the RemoteJobs44 mobile experience, sharing the **Deep Ocean** design language with the PWA in `../app`. Built with **Expo** so the same codebase ships to **Android (`.apk`/`.aab`)** and **iOS (`.ipa`)**.

## What's inside
```
_native/
├─ App.tsx              Navigation root (onboarding → auth gate → tabs + stack)
├─ app.json            Expo config (icons, splash, bundle IDs)
├─ package.json        Dependencies (Expo 51, React Navigation, AsyncStorage, SVG)
├─ assets/             icon.png · adaptive-icon.png · splash.png
└─ src/
   ├─ theme.ts         Light + dark tokens (mirrors the PWA palette)
   ├─ data.ts          Mock data + `API.*` seam · notifications · statuses · plans · AI
   ├─ store.tsx        Context + AsyncStorage (saved/applied/profile/theme/Pro/onboarding)
   ├─ ui.tsx           Icon, Logo, CompanyLogo, JobCard
   ├─ screens.tsx      Auth · Feed · Search · Detail · Applications · Saved · Profile
   ├─ extra.tsx        Onboarding · Inbox · Edit profile · Preferences · Invite
   └─ pro.tsx          Plans/Paywall · Settings · AI toolkit · Reset password
```

> The folder is named **`_native`** (leading underscore) so this project's
> design-system compiler ignores it — npm packages like React Navigation can't
> resolve in that pass. Rename to `native/` if you lift it into its own repo.

## Run it (development)
```bash
cd _native
npm install
npx expo start            # press a = Android, i = iOS, or scan the QR in Expo Go
```
> Requires Node 18+. For native runs you need Android Studio (emulator) or Xcode (simulator); or use the **Expo Go** app on a real device.

## Build store binaries (EAS)
```bash
npm install -g eas-cli
eas login
eas build:configure        # writes eas.json, sets the projectId in app.json

# Android — installable .apk for sideloading / testing:
eas build -p android --profile preview

# Android — .aab for the Play Store:
eas build -p android --profile production

# iOS — .ipa (needs an Apple Developer account):
eas build -p ios --profile production
```
The `.apk` download link is printed when the build finishes (and appears in your Expo dashboard).

> Tip: add this to `eas.json` so the preview profile emits an installable APK rather than an AAB:
> ```json
> { "build": { "preview": { "android": { "buildType": "apk" } } } }
> ```

## Wiring a real backend
Everything goes through `src/data.ts → API`. Replace the mock bodies with `fetch()`:
```ts
async listJobs(f) {
  const r = await fetch(`https://api.remotejobs44.com/jobs?` + new URLSearchParams(f as any));
  return r.json();
}
```
No screen changes needed — the UI only depends on the `API` shape and the `Job` type.

## Parity notes
- **Reconciliation:** the screens in `extra.tsx` / `pro.tsx` (onboarding, inbox,
  edit profile, job preferences, plans/paywall, settings, AI toolkit, reset
  password) plus the Pro/free daily-apply cap, 6-state application tracker,
  notification bell + badge, similar-roles and “apply on company site” close the
  gaps documented in `../uploads/MOBILE_DESIGN_RECONCILIATION.md`.
- **Fonts:** the PWA uses Sora + DM Sans (Google Fonts). Add them with `expo-font` / `@expo-google-fonts/sora` + `@expo-google-fonts/dm-sans` and set `theme.font` for pixel parity; the scaffold falls back to system fonts.
- **Company logos** load from DuckDuckGo's favicon service (same as the PWA) with a monogram fallback.
- **Persistence** uses AsyncStorage; the PWA uses localStorage. Same keys/shape conceptually.
- Screens are intentionally compact ports — extend Detail (apply sheet, share), Notifications, and CV/skills editing to match the PWA 1:1 as needed.
